<h2>The three regimes side by side</h2>
<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Provision</th><th>Who can claim</th><th>Until when</th><th>Forum</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Section 144 BNSS</td><td>Minor children, legitimate or illegitimate; adult children with physical or mental abnormality or injury</td><td>Majority; no limit where disability continues</td><td>Magistrate / Family Court</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Section 20(3) HAMA</td><td>Children of Hindu parents; specifically the unmarried daughter unable to maintain herself</td><td>Majority for sons; until marriage for unmarried daughters</td><td>Family Court / civil court</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Section 26 HMA</td><td>Children of a marriage in HMA proceedings</td><td>As the matrimonial court orders, including education provision</td><td>The matrimonial court itself</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>Why the gap exists</h2>
<p>Section 144 BNSS is a summary, secular remedy against vagrancy — deliberately narrow, quick and preventive. Parliament fixed its outer limits at minority and disability, leaving the fuller parental obligation to personal law. HAMA, by contrast, codifies the Hindu obligation to maintain children and aged parents as an incident of the relationship itself, which is why the unmarried daughter\'s entitlement survives majority there. The practical consequence: a claim struck out as not maintainable under one statute may be perfectly good under another, and nothing prevents the claimant from proceeding under the right one.</p>

<h2>Common scenarios</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>College-going son, parents divorcing.</strong> Provision for his education is sought under Section 26 HMA within the divorce; a standalone Section 144 BNSS claim in his own name will founder on majority.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Unmarried adult daughter, no matrimonial case pending.</strong> Section 20(3) HAMA petition against the father (and, on means, the mother), including marriage expenses where imminent.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Adult child with disability.</strong> Section 144 BNSS continues past majority; file the medical board certificate and plead the inability to self-maintain expressly.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Order passed during minority, child now 18.</strong> The payer applies under Section 146 BNSS for alteration; the recipient side should pre-empt by moving under HAMA or Section 26 HMA before the cushion disappears.</div>
</div>

<h2>Drafting and strategy</h2>
<div class="check">
<ul>
  <li><strong>Name the statute deliberately:</strong> pleadings that drift between "child maintenance" theories invite maintainability objections. Identify the provision, the relationship and the disqualifying or qualifying facts.</li>
  <li><strong>Prove the inability, not just the status:</strong> for the unmarried daughter, plead and prove that her own earnings and property cannot maintain her — the entitlement is conditional, not automatic.</li>
  <li><strong>Quantify education realistically:</strong> fee receipts, hostel costs and coaching expenses carry more weight than round figures; courts increasingly expect the same documentary discipline the affidavit-of-assets regime brought to spousal maintenance.</li>
  <li><strong>Watch the transition dates:</strong> an approaching eighteenth birthday is a litigation event. Both sides should calendar it.</li>
</ul>
</div>

<div class="note">
<p><strong>Practice note:</strong> These regimes are cumulative in coverage but not in recovery — the same expense cannot be collected twice under two statutes, and courts adjust overlapping awards. A consolidated family settlement that provides for the children\'s education through a defined milestone (graduation, marriage) avoids serial litigation as each threshold arrives.</p>
</div>
